>Every other type of game allows for scumming, and many times they
>encouraged it actively. ("It's a good idea to save before using this item")
>I figure that since nethack can trace it's ancestory to pre-internet days,
>perhaps there was something in the begining that made scumming bad?

All roguelikes trace their spiritual heritage back to the Unix game
'rogue', which treated the saved game file as a "I'm leaving my terminal
and want to finish my game later" file, not a "I'm doing really well
here, I'm going to checkpoint my progress so I don't have to go through
the early part of the game again" file.

And "pre-internet days" is a very long time ago indeed.  It's certainly
pre-Rogue.

>Or
>maybe I am looking at it from the wrong side. When did save scumming become
>accepted for other games?

1981 or so.  Wizardry: Proving Grounds Of The Mad Overlord made no attempt
to prevent you from copying your saved-game disk, AFAICR.  Certainly by
1985; I *know* you could copy Ultima II and III player disks (I've never
met the original instance of Ultima I, only the 1986-7 or so rerelease).

